    _cache[7] || (_cache[7] = createStaticVNode('<div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p><strong>If the development board is not recognized:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Replace the USB-C data cable. Some cables support charging only and do not support data transfer.</strong></li><li><strong>Unplug and reconnect the USB-C cable.</strong></li><li><strong>Check Device Manager for any device with an exclamation mark.</strong></li></ul></div><hr><h2 id="_1-4-install-arduino-app-lab" tabindex="-1">1.4 Install Arduino App Lab <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-4-install-arduino-app-lab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.4 Install Arduino App Lab&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Arduino App Lab</strong> is a <strong>hybrid development environment</strong> designed for UNO Q. It integrates Python and Arduino in one project and allows the two chips to work together through <strong>Router Bridge</strong>.</p><p>Open <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1BMDHwfAsRf4oxnnkh64XW4drAokY8JoR?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><strong>2. Software &amp; Programs\\01 Arduino App Lab Installation Package</strong></a> in the same directory as this document. Select the installer for the computer system, then double-click the installer. After installation is complete, the interface appears as shown below:</p><img loading="lazy" height="211" width="222" src="' + _imports_7 + '" class="common_img"><blockquote><p>If a driver installation prompt appears during installation, <strong>accept all driver installation requests</strong>. Otherwise, the development board cannot be recognized.</p></blockquote><p id="p1-5"></p><h2 id="_1-5-connect-configure-and-update" tabindex="-1">1.5 Connect, Configure, and Update <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-5-connect-configure-and-update" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.5 Connect, Configure, and Update&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Before startup, connect Arduino UNO Q to the computer with a data cable.</strong></p><ol><li>Open <strong>Arduino App Lab</strong> and wait for it to connect to the Arduino UNO Q development board.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_8 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="2"><li>Connect to the Arduino UNO Q development board.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_9 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="3"><li>Configure the Arduino UNO Q development board.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="679" width="1267" src="' + _imports_10 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="4"><li>Configure networking for the Arduino UNO Q development board. This step can be skipped, but completing it first makes later use more convenient.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="685" width="1278" src="' + _imports_11 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="5"><li>Set the Linux login password for the Arduino UNO Q development board.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="679" width="1279" src="' + _imports_12 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="6"><li>Wait for loading after configuration is complete.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_13 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="7"><li>The system detects available updates. Components in the update list can be updated. These updates are not mandatory, and skipping them does not affect current use. To use the latest version of Arduino App Lab, click <strong>Install updates</strong>.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_14 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="8"><li>Wait for the update to complete.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_15 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="9"><li>After the update is complete, click <strong>Restart APP Lab</strong>.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_16 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="10"><li>Start using App Lab.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_17 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="11"><li>App Lab home screen with official examples.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_18 + '" class="common_img"><hr><h2 id="_1-6-select-an-operating-mode" tabindex="-1">1.6 Select an Operating Mode <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-6-select-an-operating-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.6 Select an Operating Mode&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Arduino UNO Q supports three operating modes based on <strong>where App Lab runs</strong> and <strong>how the board connects</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Where App Lab runs</th><th>Connection method</th><th>Recommended use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PC Hosted Mode</strong></td><td>Computer</td><td>Direct USB-C connection</td><td>Daily development and debugging</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SBC Mode</strong> (Single-board computer mode)</td><td>UNO Q</td><td>Docking station, display, keyboard, and mouse</td><td>Standalone demonstrations and teaching</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Network Mode</strong></td><td>Computer</td><td>WiFi LAN</td><td>Remote development and maintenance</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="_1-6-1-pc-hosted-mode" tabindex="-1">1.6.1 PC Hosted Mode <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-6-1-pc-hosted-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.6.1 PC Hosted Mode&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>PC Hosted Mode</strong> is the most common development mode. App Lab is installed and runs on the computer. UNO Q connects to the computer through a USB-C data cable, which supplies power and transfers data through one cable.</p><img loading="lazy" height="351" width="599" src="' + _imports_19 + '" class="common_img"><p><strong>Workflow:</strong></p><ol><li>Connect UNO Q to a USB port on the computer with a USB-C data cable.</li><li>The on-board power indicator, PWR, lights up and UNO Q starts.</li><li>Open <strong>Arduino App Lab</strong> on the computer and wait for the development board to be recognized automatically.</li><li>After the development board appears in the device list, click it to connect and start development.</li></ol><p><strong>Key features:</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Plug and play</strong></td><td>USB-C provides both power, 5V/3A, and a data channel</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Real-time debugging</strong></td><td>View Console logs, build output, and running status in App Lab</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sufficient performance</strong></td><td>Code compilation and execution depend on the computer performance for a smooth experience</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Firmware update</strong></td><td>Flash a complete Linux system image through USB</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h3 id="_1-6-2-sbc-mode" tabindex="-1">1.6.2 SBC Mode <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-6-2-sbc-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.6.2 SBC Mode&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>SBC Mode</strong> uses UNO Q as an <strong>independent mini Linux computer</strong>. App Lab is preinstalled in the Debian system on UNO Q. Connect a display, keyboard, and mouse, then complete all development work directly on the board.</p><p><strong>Additional hardware required:</strong></p><p>UNO Q has only one USB-C port. Use a <strong>USB-C multifunction docking station</strong> to output video and connect peripherals at the same time.</p><img loading="lazy" height="469" width="736" src="' + _imports_20 + '" class="common_img"><p><strong>Docking station requirements:</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>HDMI or DisplayPort</strong></td><td>Required</td><td>UNO Q outputs video through USB-C DisplayPort Alt Mode. The docking station converts the signal to HDMI or DP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>USB-C PD power input</strong></td><td>Required</td><td>Power UNO Q through the docking station, 5V/3A</td></tr><tr><td><strong>USB-A port</strong></td><td>Required</td><td>Connect the keyboard and mouse</td></tr><tr><td>Ethernet port</td><td>Optional</td><td>Wired network connection, which is more stable than WiFi</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Startup process:</strong></p><ol><li>Connect the docking station, display, keyboard, mouse, and power supply as shown above.</li><li>After power-on, UNO Q starts Debian Linux automatically and the desktop environment appears on the display.</li><li>Enter the Linux login password to log in. The password is set during first-time configuration in <a href="#p1-5">section 1.5</a>.</li><li>Double-click the <strong>Arduino App Lab</strong> icon on the desktop to start development.</li></ol><p><strong>SBC Mode features:</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fully independent</strong></td><td>No external computer is required. UNO Q itself is a Linux host</td></tr><tr><td><strong>App Lab runs on the board</strong></td><td>All compilation and execution are completed on the QRB2210 processor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Suitable for demonstrations and teaching</strong></td><td>A board and a portable display are enough for a complete demonstration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4GB version recommended</strong></td><td>The desktop experience on the 2GB version may lag</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h3 id="_1-6-3-network-mode" tabindex="-1">1.6.3 Network Mode <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-6-3-network-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.6.3 Network Mode&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Network Mode</strong> allows remote access to UNO Q through a <strong>WiFi LAN</strong>. App Lab still runs on the computer, but it discovers and connects to the board through the network. The experience is similar to direct USB connection, with no need to plug or unplug cables before development. The prerequisite is that the computer and UNO Q are on the <strong>same LAN</strong>. If Arduino UNO Q has already been configured for SBC Mode, it can still be accessed remotely through Arduino App Lab on the computer and Network Mode. Both modes can be used at the same time.</p><p>UNO Q uses the <strong>mDNS</strong> protocol, also known as Multicast DNS, to broadcast its presence on the LAN. App Lab scans automatically and displays available Network devices.</p><p><strong>First-time configuration: Connect UNO Q to WiFi</strong></p><ol><li>Connect the development board through USB first, using PC Hosted Mode, and open App Lab.</li><li>Open <strong>Settings</strong> &gt; <strong>Wi-Fi Networks</strong>.</li><li>Select the wireless network and enter the password.</li><li>UNO Q saves the network and connects automatically after later power-on.</li></ol><p><strong>Connect with Network Mode:</strong></p><ol><li>Open Arduino App Lab on the computer. Make sure the computer and UNO Q are on the <strong>same LAN</strong>, then wait for the system to scan for UNO Q on the LAN.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="624" width="824" src="' + _imports_21 + '" class="common_img" alt="Create a new project" style="zoom:90%;"><ol start="2"><li>App Lab detects UNO Q.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="659" width="1024" src="' + _imports_22 + '" class="common_img" alt="Create a new project" style="zoom:80%;"><ol start="3"><li>Click the development board and enter the Linux login password.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="774" width="921" src="' + _imports_23 + '" class="common_img" alt="Create a new project" style="zoom:85%;"><ol start="4"><li>After connection succeeds, create projects, write code, and click <strong>Run</strong> just as with a direct USB connection.</li></ol><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p><strong>Troubleshooting:</strong> If the development board cannot be found in Network Mode:</p><ul><li><strong>Windows:</strong> When App Lab starts for the first time, Windows Defender may display a network access request for <code>mdns-discovery.exe</code>. Click <strong>Allow</strong>.</li><li><strong>Network environment:</strong> Enterprise networks, guest WiFi, VPNs, or strict firewalls may block mDNS discovery. Accessing the development board via its IP address is a good indication that Network Mode is available</li></ul></div><h2 id="_1-7-run-an-official-example" tabindex="-1">1.7 Run an Official Example <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-7-run-an-official-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.7 Run an Official Example&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This section uses the classic <strong>Blink</strong> example, with a direct USB-C connection, to demonstrate how to create and run an App Lab project.</p><p><strong>Create a new project</strong></p><ol><li>In App Lab, click <strong>Create new app</strong> or the <strong>+</strong> button.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="366" width="1919" src="' + _imports_24 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="2"><li>Enter the project name: <code>MyBlink</code>.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="251" width="650" src="' + _imports_25 + '" class="common_img"><ol start="3"><li>Click <strong>Create new</strong>.</li></ol><p>App Lab automatically generates two files: <code>main.py</code> and <code>sketch.ino</code>.</p><p><strong>Project file overview</strong></p><p>After the project is created, the following project structure appears:</p><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_26 + '" class="common_img"><p><strong>Write Python code in main.py</strong></p><p>Double-click <code>main.py</code>, delete the default content, and enter the following code:</p><div class="language-python vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">python</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">from</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> arduino.app_utils </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">import</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> *</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">if</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> __name__</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> ==</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;__main__&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    App.run()</span></span></code></pre></div><blockquote><p>The only function of <code>App.run()</code> is to start the Router Bridge, enabling communication between the Python side and the Arduino side. For a purely MCU-based Blink example, the Python side only needs to start the runtime.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Write Arduino code in sketch.ino</strong></p><p>Double-click <code>sketch.ino</code> and enter the following code:</p><div class="language-cpp vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">cpp</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">#include</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Arduino_RouterBridge.h&quot;</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">void</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> setup</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">() {</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    // Initialize the built-in LED pin</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    pinMode</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT);</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    // Start Router Bridge and connect to the Python side</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    Bridge.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">begin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">();</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">void</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> loop</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">() {</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH);</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">  // Turn on</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1000</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                       // Wait 1 second</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, LOW);</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   // Turn off</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1000</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                       // Wait 1 second</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Run the program</strong></p><p>After confirming that both files have been saved:</p><ol><li>Make sure UNO Q is connected to the computer through USB-C.</li><li>Confirm that the development board has been recognized in App Lab.</li><li>Click <strong>Run</strong> on the toolbar.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="442" width="519" src="' + _imports_27 + '" class="common_img"><p>App Lab then performs the following steps in sequence:</p><ol><li>Compile and deploy <code>sketch.ino</code> to the STM32 MCU.</li><li>Start <code>main.py</code> on the QRB2210 Linux side.</li><li>Establish the Router Bridge communication link.</li></ol><p>After deployment succeeds, the <strong>LED3 red channel on UNO Q starts blinking at 1 second intervals</strong>. The complete project workflow from creation to execution is now finished.</p><h2 id="_1-8-copy-and-modify-an-example" tabindex="-1">1.8 Copy and Modify an Example <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-8-copy-and-modify-an-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.8 Copy and Modify an Example&quot;">​</a></h2><p id="p1-8-1"></p><h3 id="_1-8-1-program-download" tabindex="-1">1.8.1 Program Download <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-8-1-program-download" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.8.1 Program Download&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Based on the official Blink example, modify the code to produce different effects. Starting from this section, case code is provided directly. Import and use the case code from the product materials. Before that, quickly learn how to import a project and download the program.</p><ol><li>Connect the <strong>Arduino UNO Q</strong> development board to the PC with a <strong>Type-C</strong> data cable, as shown below:</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="971" width="1619" src="' + _imports_0$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:600px;"><ol start="2"><li>Open <strong>Arduino App Lab</strong>. After the device starts successfully, click the <strong>Arduino UNO Q</strong> development board icon to connect.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="708" width="739" src="' + _imports_1$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:600px;"><ol start="3"><li>Click the <strong>My Apps</strong> icon in the upper-left corner to import the project.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="761" width="1010" src="' + _imports_2$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:600px;"><ol start="4"><li>Use the <strong>mcu_telemetry</strong> project as an example. Drag the corresponding project zip package into the window.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="761" width="1010" src="' + _imports_3$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:600px;"><ol start="5"><li>After the import success prompt appears, click the corresponding project icon to enter the project.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="761" width="1010" src="' + _imports_4$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:600px;"><ol start="6"><li>In the project, click the <strong>Run</strong> button in the upper-right corner to run the current project.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="761" width="1010" src="' + _imports_5$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:600px;"><ol start="7"><li>To run the program automatically after the development board powers on, configure it as shown below.</li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="761" width="1010" src="' + _imports_6$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:600px;"><h3 id="_1-8-2-modify-blink-timing-1" tabindex="-1">1.8.2 Modify Blink Timing 1 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-8-2-modify-blink-timing-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.8.2 Modify Blink Timing 1&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Program Download</strong></p><p>Find the <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1asduVNKJuP9URf5f1c5UPn9TGHINRxK8?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><strong>2. Software &amp; Programs\\02 Source Code\\1. Quick Start\\1.MyBlink_speed01.zip</strong></a> source package in the same directory as this document and import it. Refer to <a href="#p1-8-1">1.8.1 Program Download</a> for the detailed download procedure.</p><p><strong>Project Outcome</strong></p><p>After the program is uploaded to Arduino UNO Q, the on-board LED blinks quickly. The effect is more obvious than the original 1 second interval. Specifically, the LED turns on and off rapidly at 0.2 second intervals, blinking about 2.5 times per second for a fast flashing effect.</p><p><strong>Program Analysis</strong></p><p>The header file imports the RouterBridge library to connect MCU and Linux communication:</p><div class="language-cpp vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">cpp</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">#include</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Arduino_RouterBridge.h&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>In <code>setup</code>, configure the LED pin and start Bridge:</p><div class="language-cpp vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">cpp</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">pinMode</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">Bridge.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">begin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">();</span></span></code></pre></div><p>In <code>loop</code>, the four lines of code form one blink cycle. The 200 ms delay determines the blink speed:</p><div class="language-cpp vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">cpp</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH);</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">  // Turn on</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">200</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, LOW);</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   // Turn off</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">200</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="_1-8-3-modify-blink-timing-2" tabindex="-1">1.8.3 Modify Blink Timing 2 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-8-3-modify-blink-timing-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.8.3 Modify Blink Timing 2&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Program Download</strong></p><p>Find the <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1asduVNKJuP9URf5f1c5UPn9TGHINRxK8?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><strong>2. Software &amp; Programs\\02 Source Code\\1.Quick Start\\2.MyBlink_speed02.zip</strong></a> source package in the same directory as this document and import it. Refer to <a href="#p1-8-1">1.8.1 Program Download</a> for the detailed download procedure.</p><p><strong>Project Outcome</strong></p><p>The LED repeatedly plays the SOS distress signal: three short blinks, three long blinks, and three short blinks. After the full sequence, it pauses for 1 second and repeats.</p><p><strong>Program Analysis</strong></p><p>The S signal uses a <code>for</code> loop to create three quick blinks. Each on and off duration is 200 ms:</p><div class="language-cpp vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">cpp</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">for</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">int</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> i </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; i </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 3</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; i</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">++</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">) {</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">200</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, LOW);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">200</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The O signal also loops three times, but the on duration is extended to 600 ms to create long blinks:</p><div class="language-cpp vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">cpp</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">for</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">int</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> i </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; i </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 3</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; i</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">++</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">) {</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">600</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    digitalWrite</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(LED_BUILTIN, LOW);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">    delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">200</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Use <code>delay(300)</code> to separate letters. Use <code>delay(1000)</code> after the full sequence before the next loop.</p><h2 id="_1-9-view-the-app-page-and-console-logs" tabindex="-1">1.9 View the App Page and Console Logs <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-9-view-the-app-page-and-console-logs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.9 View the App Page and Console Logs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>App Lab home screen navigation</strong></p><p>After App Lab starts, the left sidebar contains the following function areas:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Navigation item</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>My Apps</strong></td><td>View, edit, and manage created or copied apps</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Examples</strong></td><td>Browse built-in and ready-made Arduino projects</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bricks</strong></td><td>Explore modular code blocks, such as web servers or AI models</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Learn</strong></td><td>Access offline documents and tutorials</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Settings</strong></td><td>Manage preferences, including Wi-Fi networks, Linux password, and keyboard layout</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Account</strong></td><td>View Arduino account settings</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>App Lab home screen:</strong></p><img loading="lazy" height="1033" width="1919" src="' + _imports_35 + '" class="common_img"><p><strong>View Console logs and output</strong></p><p>The output panel at the bottom of App Lab displays program logs, build information, and error messages. When a program has a problem, <strong>check the Console output first</strong> to locate the issue quickly.</p><img loading="lazy" height="762" width="1215" src="' + _imports_36 + '" class="common_img"><h2 id="_1-10-faqs" tabindex="-1">1.10 FAQs <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-10-faqs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1.10 FAQs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Q1: Nothing happens after clicking Run, and the LED does not light up</strong></p><blockquote><p><strong>Check:</strong></p><ol><li>Check whether the USB-C cable is securely connected. Try using another cable, such as a charging cable or a data cable.</li><li>Confirm that the pin has been initialized with <code>pinMode(pin, OUTPUT)</code>.</li><li>Check whether the output panel at the bottom of App Lab shows any error messages.</li></ol></blockquote><p><strong>Q2: Bridge.call() reports &quot;function not found&quot;</strong></p><blockquote><p><strong>Cause:</strong> The Python side does not expose the function with <code>Bridge.provide()</code>, or the function names are inconsistent.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong> Make sure <code>Bridge.provide(&quot;xxx&quot;, xxx)</code> on the Python side and <code>Bridge.call(&quot;xxx&quot;)</code> on the Arduino side use exactly the same name.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Q3: Compilation reports &quot;Arduino_RouterBridge.h not found&quot;</strong></p><blockquote><p><strong>Cause:</strong> App Lab did not load the Router Bridge library correctly.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong> Make sure <strong>Arduino UNO Q</strong> is selected as the target device when creating the project. App Lab automatically adds the required library dependencies.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Q4: How do changes on the Python side take effect?</strong></p><blockquote><p>Click <strong>Run</strong> to redeploy code on both sides. If only <code>main.py</code> is modified, App Lab may support <code>hot reload</code> without reflashing the MCU, but a complete <strong>Run</strong> is recommended each time.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Q5: Can Arduino IDE replace App Lab for development?</strong></p><blockquote><p>Yes. However, App Lab hybrid development capability, Python + Bridge, will not be available. For pure MCU programming, Arduino IDE is simpler. See the <a href="https://docs.arduino.cc/software/ide/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Arduino IDE development guide</a>.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Q6: Can App Lab projects be shared with Arduino IDE?</strong></p><blockquote><p>The <code>.ino</code> file can be shared, but <code>main.py</code> is specific to App Lab. To open the project in Arduino IDE, import only <code>sketch.ino</code>, and remove <code>#include &quot;Arduino_RouterBridge.h&quot;</code> and <code>Bridge.begin()</code>.</p></blockquote>', 159))
